==Trivia==
*Cat Clan Heroes consists of multiple anthropomorphic versions of Cat Units: [[Axe Cat (Normal Cat)|Brave Cat]], [[Viking Cat (Rare Cat)|Viking Cat]], [[Bishop Cat (Rare Cat)|Bishop Cat]] and [[Witch Cat (Rare Cat)|Witch Cat]] in the Normal Form, who are joined by [[Thief Cat (Rare Cat)|Thief Cat]], [[Cat Gunslinger (Rare Cat)|Cat Gunslinger]], [[Onmyoji Cat (Rare Cat)|Onmyoji Cat]], [[Swordsman Cat (Rare Cat)|Sword Master Cat]], [[Pirate Cat (Rare Cat)|Captain Cat]], [[Fortune Teller Cat (Rare Cat)|Fortune Teller Cat]], [[Jurassic Cat (Rare Cat)|Jurassic Cat]] and [[Shaman Cat (Rare Cat)|Shaman Cat]] in the Evolved and True Forms.
**All of the Cats seem to evolve again in the True Form, except for the human version of Brave Cat, who bears no resemblance to Dark Cat.
**The characters presented in its Evolved and True Form represent Rare Cats with a similar ability ([[:Category:Anti-Red Cats|anti-Red]], [[:Category:Anti-Floating Cats|anti-Floating]], [[:Category:Anti-Dark Cats|anti-Dark]] and [[:Category:Critical Hit Cats|Critical Hitter]]), while the Normal Form only has anti-Red and anti-Floating.
*All the heroes are based on Rare Cats from the [[Cat Capsule#Rare Capsule|Rare Cat Capsules]], except the "Chosen One", who is a [[:Category:Normal Cats|Normal Cat]].
*Along with [[Lasvoss (Uber Rare Cat)|Lasvoss]], the Cat Clan Heroes are inspired by JRPGs, especially ''[[wikipedia:Dragon Quest|Dragon Quest]]''.
**The "Chosen One" in the first form resembles the [https://dragon-quest.org/wiki/Hero_(Dragon_Quest_III) Hero] from ''[[wikia:dragonquest:Dragon Quest III|Dragon Quest III]]''.
**Cat Quest Heroes riding an airship is likely a reference to the [[wikia:finalfantasy:Airship (term)|airships]] of the ''[[wikipedia:Final Fantasy|Final Fantasy]]'' series.
**The line “ Heroes given the divine gift to cross time and save the world” refers the Dragon Quest VII, where the player time travels to save pieces of the world from the demon king.
**Cat Clan Heroes' ability to do [[Critical Hit]]s is likely a reference to how player characters in JRPGs usually have a low chance to land Critical Hits.
**The structure in Heavenly Cat Heroes' design could be a reference to [https://dragonquest-wiki.com/Zenithia Zenithia], a sky castle that prominently appears in the fourth and fifth entries of the ''Dragon Quest'' series that also acts as the second means of flight in DQV.
*The "King of Evil"/"Demon King" mentioned in their Normal Form's English/Japanese descriptions may be Lasvoss or [[Infernal Tyrant Nyandam]]. Further proof that it could be Lasvoss is that in his description it states that he 'smites adventurers and destroys heroes' who could be cat clan heroes.
*They are the first Uber Rare Cat whose [[True Form]] requires [[Catfruit]] Seeds. This may be because they consist of humanoid versions of different Rare Cats, which in turn also require Seeds and few fruits.
*Cat Clan Heroes' Normal, Evolved and True Forms had slightly different descriptions before [[Version 15.3 Update|Version 15.3]]:
**In English, they were changed to have their targeted traits separated by slashes rather than commas.
**In Japanese, they were changed to have [[:Category:Floating Enemies|Floating]] listed before [[:Category:Dark Enemies|Dark]], and their True Form now has the traits separated by と rather than commas.

